int  because nearly all the main characters  have died and come back it just sucks  all the stakes out of the drama there's  no tension when somebody is either left  in a situation where they're probably  gonna die or just flat out killed you're  like okay are they gonna come back too  it's way overused and like even beyond  the idea if they establish one character  as the power of Resurrection again it's  done by so many people and or things  that where the character maybe does in  fact die but is functionally still  around like Spalding  death has no meaning in this season none  and then there's sort of just a general  overall issue I've had with most seasons  of American Horror Story that I've seen  which is that the rules are not very  well established and they feel very  loose it either feels like the writers  know what the rules are but they're not  going to let the audience in on enough  of them that we can keep up with what's  happening because things keep happening  that feel like shouldn't that break the  rules that were established before or it  feels like they're just making things up  as they go along I felt that about as I  said nearly every season I didn't really  feel that necessarily about freak Show  but that is one of the more uh grounded  and least Supernatural ones at least of  the seasons that I have seen and I would  actually even argue that it worked in  Asylum because again when you're dealing  with this idea of the perception of  reality and can you even trust your own  brain or can any of the characters trust  theirs doing things that appear to break  the rules feels like it goes with the  flow much more than being something it's  like wait how does this work again  that's something that I felt in murder  house where I felt like I was three  quarters of the way in still trying to  figure out how the rules of these ghosts  worked but in Coven it's that oh to the  third power at least where they'll  introduce rules about like everyone has  o